all right we are speaking with lola  brooks she is currently the  president of the clark county school  district board of trustees  her seat is district e and she is  running  once again for the seat this is the  second time you're running  and talk to me about why did you want to  run  again i think that this election cycle  is very interesting because there are  four seats that are up for election and  three of us  are termed out and i didn't feel that  leaving a majority of the board as  inexperienced incoming board members  would be good  i felt it would be a loss of  institutional knowledge and i felt that  the experience would be necessary to  help usher us through this process i  wanted to ask you about that  how do you think this election will  really change the makeup of the board  going forward  it's hard to say um i am always  optimistic  i think everybody brings a different  type of value  so i'm excited about that part i'm  excited to learn  what the different things are that some  of the candidates bring  but it will be an adjustment period  because we do have some  long-term board members leaving the  board for you  what are the issues that are important  right now that you want constituents to  know  going into this election november 3rd  i think that people should be aware of  what the board does  as far as what our role is and then i  want to make sure that people take the  time to research candidates so that they  are  as prepared as possible to hit the  ground running in january as we're  possibly transitioning into  schools or improving distance education  or whatever that looks like  so experience really really matters this  election cycle  in other times when you're not in a  global pandemic  some other board members can counter  that but i don't know that that's going  to be possible this cycle  i know that's really something that your  platform is on you have the experience  you've been president for two years  in regards to the uncertainty going  forward we are in the middle  of distance education a lot of families  not a big  fan so and as you mentioned trying to  figure out that transition  how necessary is it for you to want to  continue being a part of that  as we move forward i think that  it's really interesting uh with my  background and how that ties into this  role because i actually work in a school  that was already blended learning and  they transitioned to distance education  so  i've seen some of those struggles from  like a technology point of view  and it's it's really frustrating i have  a 13 year old who's in eighth grade and  i  am experiencing the same exact problems  as parents  and i also sympathize with teachers  because i know this is a difficult  transition so  trying to find a balance between um  making sure our expectations  and we are forward thinking we're trying  to fix we're trying to find solutions  and making sure that we're also having  realistic expectations because this is  a really challenging time for everyone  do you think  adding new members can really help the  dynamic of the board because  we have seen over the summer there were  issues we had the special meeting  discussing  jarrah's employment with the district so  and we  did see some split opinion so how do you  think  this election may help either unify the  board or maybe continue on the same path  it's been on  i think it's possible the election can  serve as a unifying event  it really depends on who else is elected  and why they chose to run  if candidates are elected that are  primarily in it for publicity  or for you know  self self promotion then it's really  really hard because they  tend to enter the field and they want to  make an impact right away  and sometimes you need to take a step  back and learn what your role is before  you can truly make a difference  and it that's really going to be what  would cause a challenge but  i think everybody will be closely  watching and  because of that i think there will be a  tendency of  people to maybe model better behavior  from the get-go  who do you feel is running for the  publicity  i would never ever ever um  talk negatively about a fellow board  member or a candidate running for office  i don't think that's professional  in regards to you running critics may  say  that um there's been some intense  moments on the board and they've seen  you  seem to align more so with dr jara  than not so how do you respond to people  who may be worried that  you are too aligned with him and maybe  not  formulating your own opinions or  standing up for what you think should be  done  i am i've always been outspoken and i've  always been direct and honest  so dr jar when he does disappoint me he  does hear it  uh i do believe in making sure that you  provide feedback  to your employees at the end of the day  dr jarrah is one of my employees  and i would never want to publicly or  privately treat  my employee my only employee in a way  that  is negative or is not helpful and i  would never provide  criticism that is not meant to be  constructive  so i i think that there is a tendency  in the community for people to want to  see us as adversaries but we're actually  on the same team  we have the same goals and the key  is providing direct feedback and making  sure that people understand what you  expect of them  and helping them reach that because  that's  that's who i am as a leader is there  anything else you'd like to add just  about this race and the fact you are the  only incumbent that has actually been  very  interesting i i've never run for  re-election before so  i don't have anything compared to but it  is very strange being the only incumbent  as  there are three uh new people definitely  coming on board  i just want to make sure that the public  does a lot of research on candidates  and that the candidates that are  watching also do a lot of research as  far as what their roles are  and the expectations so that they can be  better prepared and equipped for the  role  come january and lastly distance  education i know a lot  of parents may be thinking about that as  they go into the polls  where do you want to see that going i  know right now there are discussions  happening behind the scenes  so if you can maybe give parents any  insight about  any hope what may be going on and your  viewpoint on it  i think that there are lots of people  struggling with distance education  um i my family is one of them and we  should definitely  try to work out the kinks that they're  experiencing as soon as possible  our priority is health and community  health but we can't  we can't sacrifice the mental health and  the um i just don't know how sustainable  distance education is long term so we  have to  make some improvements so that we can we  can see it  last longer but we also have to be  willing to talk about transition  planning because some  some students are not going to be  successful in this environment  which we're starting to see already yes  right anything else you'd like to add  no i am good uh thank you for the invite  i appreciate it
 

